    {
      "id": "claim_3ba7bcd7dc334d36b75914239c8cb264",
      "statement": "Revocation is lazy and non-destructive: removing a collaborator deletes only the edges granting them access (owner severs all incoming edges, a non-owner only their own) while edges where they are the sharer stay untouched; revoking a link sets the revoked flag (record and edges intact, copies deleted outright since no edge references an alias); dependents simply become unreachable and are denied at open(), and re-adding restores them transitively — which is what makes lazy revocation safe, since the live effective-role computation is the sole access authority.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/sharing.md#L85-L96",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2f6bf36f07b76a4e3ec5041d1818b607f22565e83210b39dc03d90dd8e773da3:"
        },
        {
